  {
    "insert": "While quite short, the motivation is okay to introduce the reader to the topic. However, the Research Questions need a bit of work. For instance, Sub-RQ1.1 has a focus on the technical implementation. Most of the time, if you are not creating a new artifact, for instance, we do not care too much about it. If the interaction with an AI assistant is the prominent topic in your work, the technology used is just a piece of information. In contrast, if you want to build a new sort of AR glasses, this is completely different. Also, Sub-RQ1.2 came by surprise, as to this point in the paper, the reader was not introduced to this being a topic of the paper. It randomly pops in and is not explained or introduced. Finally, Sub-RQ1.3 could need a bit more detail on what is meant by “how do they respond”?\n\nThe Approach does introduce something interesting, as here the author reveals that the AI model should also be able to reproduce and create emotions. This increases the complexity drastically. Also, a bit more details on what exactly the agent is supposed to do are needed. Is it tasked to answer questions, tell a story, …?\n\nThe methodology lacks reasoning. For instance, why are expert interviews conducted? In the current text, there are just expert interviews, and then we built something. What does the author want to find in the expert interviews, and how will these findings be integrated into building the prototype? Also, the terminology could need some improvement. Instead of calling it “testing phase”, it should be a preliminary study or user study. Also, in this chapter, the privacy concern is a bit surprising. While the chapter does a better job of reasoning than the motivation, it is still somewhat surprising and feels artificially attached."
  }
]